{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Input LUT}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Input_LUT}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} EHT Off @ Log Off}
\b\fs36\cf1 Input LUT
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par This Input\b  L\plain\fs24 ook \b U\plain\fs24 p \b T\plain\fs24 able (LUT) controls the transformation of the input signal before it reaches the store. Four different transformations can be applied:
\par 
\par \b\fs28 Transparent:
\par \plain\fs24 The input LUT is set to linear so that the signal passes through the LUT unchanged.
\par 
\par \b\fs28 Gamma:
\par \plain\f13\fs24 Gamma correction changes the LUT relationship between the input signal and the output signal. It can be used to expand the contrast in an image if much of the image detail is contained in a small number of grey levels.\f4 
\par \pard The input LUT is set according to the gamma parameter. Select the parameter with the \ul Scroll Bar\plain\fs24 {\v 2HOSDE@Sem.HLP} below. 
\par For parameter input see also \uldb Parameter Control Analogue\plain\fs24 {\v 129F2JR@Sem.HLP}.
\par 
\par \b\fs28 Inverse:
\par \plain\fs24 The input LUT is set to inverted linear so that the signal passes through the LUT with inverted contrast.
\par 
\par \b\fs28 User:
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx8495\cellx8995\cellx13185\pard\intbl \plain\f13\fs24 The User setting changes the LUT relationship between the input signal and the output signal. It can be used to expand the contrast in an image if much of the image detail is contained in a small number of grey levels.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl A LUT Editor Panel opens and allows to setup a user designed LUT.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl For further information see \uldb \b Look Up Table Editor\plain\f13\b\fs24 {\v Contents@LutMgr.HLP}\plain\f13\fs24 .\cell\pard \pard\intbl \cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm12.bmp\}
\par\intbl \cell\intbl\row
\pard \page
