{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} X-Ray Dot Map: Element Priority}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} X_Ray_Dot_Map__Element_Priority}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \f4\b\fs36\cf1 X-Ray Dot Map: Element Priority
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par In this mode X-Ray counts are accumulated on each X-Ray port (energy window) for each pixel. If the count is non zero then the associated bit in the image store pixel is set, making a pixel \b DOT\plain\fs24  of the selected colour.
\par 
\par When a pixel contains non zero counts on two or more ports (energy windows) then the one displayed is determined by the presence priority which may be altered as required.
\par 
\par The Element Priority window is a box containing all the identities of the data sources. This window determines the priority of the presence dot when two or more occur in the same pixel. The top of the box is the highest priority. 
\par \pard 
\par \{bmc bm1.bmp\} The priority may be changed by selecting an entry with the mouse and the moving it with the buttons \b Move Up\plain\fs24  and \b Move Down\plain\fs24  to the required priority position.
\par 
\par \{bmc bm4.bmp\} Once the data has been collected the priority may be rearranged interactively to see the effect on the display. This is non destructive to the X-Ray data.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} X-Ray Dot Map: Energy Window Setup}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} X_Ray_Dot_Map__Energy_Window_Setup}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 X-Ray Dot Map: Energy Window Setup
\par \pard\tx355 \plain\fs24 
\par \{bmc bm1.bmp\} Select the energy window by \ul clicking\plain\fs24 {\v CLICK@Sem.HLP} on the check box \{bmc bm15.bmp\}.\tab \tab \{bmc bm28.bmp\}
\par 
\par \{bmc bm8.bmp\} Enter the identifier text into the edit box (replace WIN1 etc.). Just click the cursor in the edit box and the text may be edited in the normal way (e.g. enter the chemical symbol). This new description will be actioned when the mouse is clicked anywhere else in the X-Ray panel.
\par 
\par Each X-Ray Port's data may be displayed in one of a number of colours. The colour is shown in the field next to the label. The colour may be changed by clicking on \{bmc bm29.bmp\} or clicking the coloured area and selecting the preferred colour from the colour selection panel.
\par \pard\tx355 
\par \{bmc bm4.bmp\} \b Advice
\par \plain\fs24 It is important to choose the correct colours for dot maps if they are to be output to monochrome video printers, Grey TIFF files (as seen) or clipboard 
\par 
\par Recommended colours are:
\par 
\par \b One map\plain\fs24  \tab white
\par \b Two maps\plain\fs24  \tab white, dark red.
\par \b Three maps\plain\fs24 \tab white, orange, green
\par \b Four maps\plain\fs24 \tab white, dark red, magenta, dark green
