{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} SEM Control Signal}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} SEM_Control_Signal}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 SEM Control Signal
\par \pard \plain\fs24 This tab provides the Auto signal level functions
\par \trowd\trgaph105\trleft-116 \cellx6105\cellx9195\pard\intbl Brightness and contrast can also be controlled directly from this panel using the slider bars labelled accordingly.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl There are two types of auto signal level function which each operate in two ways. The auto signal level function will either control the \b Mean\plain\fs24  signal level or the \b Peak\plain\fs24  signal level. The mean signal level is defined as being the average brightness of all the pixels in the image, while the peak level is defined as the brightness of the brightest part of the image.
\par\intbl 
\par\intbl \b Peak\plain\fs24  and \b mean\plain\fs24  signal level controls each operate in two ways. The \b Find\plain\fs24  function is a single shot activation which sets the peak or mean to the level defined in the associated Auto Video slider bar. The \b Keep\plain\fs24  function activates the auto video function to maintain the video level (peak or mean) of the image at the time of selection.
\par\intbl Typically the \b Find\plain\fs24  function will be used to set the required video level if the image is a long way from the required brightness. The \b Keep\plain\fs24  function is useful when the content of the image is likely to change, for example if the stage is being moved across a specimen with distinct light and dark phases, or if the probe current is being adjusted.
\par\intbl Auto video control can be disabled at any time by selecting the \b  Off\plain\fs24  buttons.\cell\pard \pard\intbl \{bmc bm31.bmp\}\cell\intbl\row
\pard 
\par \page
