{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Frame Average Mode}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Frame_Average_Mode}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard \b\fs36\cf1 Frame Average Mode
\par \plain\fs24 
\par When frame average is selected the live signal is proportionally mixed with the store signal so that the image reflects the average of the recent frames. 
\par 
\par  The proportion of live to stored can be adjusted with the parameter \b N\plain\fs24  which represents the number of frames to be averaged. 
\par 
\par  Frame average can be selected with any \uldb \b scan speed\plain\b\fs24 {\v scan_speed} \plain\fs24 , but is generally most useful at the faster speeds  where a larger amount of noise reduction can be obtained without introducing a long cycle time.
\par \pard 
\par There is one important difference between line and frame average. In line average mode the same noise reduction is applied to all the active zones. But for frame average it is possible to set different frame counts for each zone.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Line Average Mode}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Line_Average_Mode}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard \b\fs36\cf1 Line Average Mode
\par \plain\fs24 
\par Selecting the \b line average\plain\fs24  causes each line to be scanned a number of times before the scan moves on.  The average line signal is stored and displayed.
\par 
\par The Noise reduction parameter \b N\plain\fs24  defines the number of times a line is to be averaged before moving to the next line
\par 
\par Line average is used when the result of the noise reduction needs to be seen without waiting for the cycle to complete.  
\par 
\par There is one important difference between line and frame average. In line average mode the same noise reduction is applied to all the active zones. But for frame average it is possible to set different frame counts for each zone.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Low KV Anode}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Low_KV_Anode}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Low KV Anode
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The acceleration voltage range of the SEM is 300V to 30kV. Although the instrument may be operated over the entire acceleration voltage range with the normal high kV anode installed, better gun geometry and brightness and hence better instrument performance is usually obtained with the anode lifted towards the grid whenever an acceleration voltage of 3kV or less is selected. 
\par This is achieved using the low kV anode, as follows:-
\par \pard\li355\fi-355\tx355 \f7 \{bmc bm32.bmp\}\f4 \tab Admit air to the gun. 
\par \f7 \{bmc bm32.bmp\}\f4 \tab  Open the gun.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm32.bmp\}\f4 \tab  Lift out the anode.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm32.bmp\}\f4 \tab  Fit the low kV anode.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm32.bmp\}\f4 \tab  Select \b Low kV spacer\plain\fs24  in the SEM Control Gun panel - This will automatically restrict the accelerating voltage
\par \f7 \{bmc bm32.bmp\}\f4 \tab  Close the gun, pump down, select the desired low voltage and obtain an image.
\par \f7 \{bmc bm32.bmp\}\f4 \tab 
\par \pard\tx355 \{bmc bm18.bmp\}\tab \b\cf2 Warning:\plain\fs24\cf2  \b The SEM  should not be used at an acceleration voltage higher than 3kV with the low kV anode fitted.  It is important to ensure that the state of the anode spacer parameter reflects the state of the machine.\plain\fs24 
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Filament Saturation}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Filament_Saturation}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Filament Saturation
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The setting of the filament current will have a large effect on the brightness and stability of the image, as well as influencing the lifetime of the filament. As the filament current is increased the brightness of the signal increases until a small plateau is reached, this is known as the "first peak" (see Figure), increasing the filament further will again cause the signal brightness to increase until another plateau is reached, further increase of the filament current will not improve the signal brightness, this is referred to as the fully saturated condition. The lifetime of the filament decreases as the filament current increases, there is no advantage in increasing the filament current above the just saturated condition. 
\par \pard 
\par \pard\qc\sb115\sa115\li2155 \f6\fs20 \{bmc bm33.wmf\}
\par \pard \f4\fs24 The filament should be operated at one of two conditions, either at the first peak setting or where the filament has become just fully saturated.  These two positions will provide conditions for stable brightness operation. Operating at the first peak setting will give a long filament life, but is not satisfactory for high resolution imaging, while the fully saturated condition will give the brightest signal and allow the best resolution images to be obtained, but the lifetime of the filament will be reduced.
\par \pard 
\par The filament current may be varied by using the associated scroll bar within the \uldb \b SEM Control Gun\plain\b\fs24 {\v SEM_Control_Gun}\plain\fs24  tab, either by SLOWLY dragging the slider in the appropriate direction, or by clicking over the arrow at the appropriate end of the scroll bar while observing the points at which maximum brightness occurs.  The two peaks of maximum brightness correspond to the two saturation peaks.
\par 
\par This procedure should be carried out in conjunction with \uldb \b Beam Alignment\plain\b\fs24 {\v Beam_Alignment}\plain\fs24   if the optimum settings are to be achieved
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Beam Alignment}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Beam_Alignment}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Beam Alignment
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The beam is correctly aligned down the column when the brightness level of the signal cannot be increased any further (ensuring the system is switched to MANUAL brightness control) by any adjustment of the gun align screws.  These are moved as opposite pairs where one is slackened and the other screwed in.  Both pairs should be adjusted against each other until the signal brightness is at a maximum.  This adjustment should be carried out in conjunction with the \uldb \b filament current adjustment\plain\b\fs24 {\v Filament_Saturation}\plain\fs24 , if the optimum settings are to be achieved.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Optibeam}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Optibeam}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn {\up K}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up K} Go To Next Point}
\b\fs36\cf1 Optibeam
\par \pard \plain\fs24 The \i Optibeam\plain\fs24  program takes the requirements for probe current, working distance, etc. and determines the optimum lens settings to achieve the best performance from the column. Usually the best performance is defined as the highest resolution, but \i Optibeam\plain\fs24  can be set to other modes as described below.
\par 
\par \b Optibeam On
\par \plain\fs24 If enabled \i Optibeam\plain\fs24  is \b On\plain\fs24 , which means that the lens currents will be calculated to give the best performance after each change to the operating conditions. When disabled the lenses can be controlled manually. The instrument is normally operated with \i Optibeam\plain\fs24  \b On\plain\f14\fs20 .\f4\b\fs24 
\par \pard \plain\fs24 
\par \b Optibeam Depth Mode
\par \plain\fs24 If this is enabled \i Optibeam \plain\fs24 automatically adjusts the lenses to obtain the largest depth of field for the operating conditions being used. If disabled, \i Optibeam\plain\fs24  automatically adjusts the lenses for optimum resolution performance for the prevailing operating conditions.
\par 
\par \b Optibeam Auto Resolution Mode 
\par \plain\fs24 If this is selected \i Optibeam\plain\fs24  automatically controls the \uldb probe current\plain\fs24 {\v 115R0I@operation.hlp} (I\'a0Probe) to give operating conditions consistent with the magnification so that the resolution is not compromised by the spot size being too large.
\par \pard 
\par \b Optibeam Conjugate mode
\par \plain\fs24 This is a special mode used when Electrostatic Beam Blanking is fitted. For operation see the EBBU documentation
\par \b 
\par \plain\fs24\cf2 
\par 
\par Depth mode is not available in conjugate mode.
\par \page
{\up $}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up $} Survey Overview}
{\up #}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up #} Survey_Overview}
{\up >}{\footnote\pard\plain{\up >} main}
\pard\keepn \b\fs36\cf1 Survey Overview
\par \pard \plain\b\fs24 Survey Mode\plain\fs24  provides the facility to enter a defined state [\uldb \b Survey Mode State\plain\b\fs24 {\v Survey_Mode}\plain\fs24 ] (typically a long Working Distance and low magnification to achieve a wide field of view) in which the Specimen may be reviewed for features of interest.
\par 
\par While in \b Survey Mode State\plain\fs24  if a \uldb \b Centre Point\plain\b\fs24 {\v N9YIPC@stage.hlp}\plain\fs24  function is executed the software, in addition to moving the selected point to centre screen, switches to another defined state [\uldb \b Resolution Mode state\plain\b\fs24 {\v Resolution_Mode}\plain\fs24 ] (typically a short working distance and higher magnification for better resolution) for examination of the point of interest.
\par \pard 
\par Similarly if a \uldb \b Centre Feature\plain\b\fs24 {\v T2YJNK@stage.hlp}\plain\fs24  function is executed while in \b Survey Mode State\plain\fs24  the software, in addition to moving the area selected to the centre of the screen and increasing magnification to fill the image, switches to \b Resolution Mode state\plain\fs24 .
\par 
\par If in \b Resolution State\plain\fs24  the Key \b <F4> \plain\fs24 will return to \b Survey Mode state\plain\fs24 .
\par 
\par Switching off \b Survey Mode\plain\fs24  returns to the conditions which existed when \b Survey Mode\plain\fs24  was switched On.
\par \pard 
\par To set up conditions for Survey Mode State and Resolution Mode State display the \uldb \b Survey Settings Dialog\plain\b\fs24 {\v Survey_Settings_Dialog}\plain\fs24  from the Interface Main Menu \b Stage - Survey Mode - Settings
\par 
\par See also \uldb Preparations for using Survey Mode\plain\b\fs24 {\v Preparations_for_using_Survey_Mode}\plain\fs24 
\par \page
